steps.instructions:
Interview first, criteria second, code last: write the criteria from what was asked, not from the code that exists. Use keel's spec template (its path and sections are above). It starts with the six sections a reader scans first: In plain words (5 to 10 short sentences for a colleague who does not know the code: what is wrong or missing, what we will do, what a user will notice, what stays the same, what we still need to find out; simple English, no file names or jargon, one everyday example), For engineers (8 to 15 precise lines: the change at each layer, inbound, parsing, the message contract, the handler, persistence, outbound, with the classes and tables named; the design choices and why: idempotency, ordering, null semantics, transaction scope; compatibility: rolling deploy, old messages, old clients; where it can fail and how we see it: logs, metrics), What will change (one line per file or area: new, changed or removed), Payload changes (JSON before and after, each field marked // + // ~ // -, the compatibility note; or "none: <the reason>"), Database changes (the table before and after with types, nullability and indexes marked + ~ -, the migration's name with its up and down SQL, online or locking on this database with the row count when known, any backfill and its cost, the deploy order migration then consumer then producer, the rollback and the data it loses, the repository or query methods that change; or "none: <the reason>"), Architecture (an ASCII request path inbound → app → domain → persistence → outbound, each box marked new, changed or unchanged, and one line on why it fits the layers). Then a Goal, User stories ("As a <role>, I want <capability>, so that <benefit>"), Findings (evidence) with a file:line or URL behind every claim and the paths the change touches traced (inbound, internal messages and their contract, persistence, outbound) with the tests that exist today, criteria "AC-n [API|WEB|E2E|SMOKE] Given, when, then" that cover absent, valid, malformed, a later update, an old message still queued and the docs, Test layers for every criterion, Validation and security rules, Edge cases, Risks and cost (files, migration, rolling deploy order, ambiguities to decide), Unverified and open questions for anything no source confirms, Out of scope, and a Definition of done. Draw before you finish the list: a UI mockup with four states (default, empty, loading, error) for any [WEB] criterion, a request path (each hop marked + new or ~ changed) for any [API] one; a schema change gets its own migration notes. Tag a journey that needs a browser [E2E] and a check that must also pass after a deploy [SMOKE]. Each interview answer becomes a sentence under Decisions; do not ask what the interview answered. When the criteria are listed above already (handed over by a change flow), do not ask questions: write the spec around them, keep their ids and words, and leave the finished ones finished. Outside docs (0.18.21): the web research below is what was confirmed about the third-party APIs, webhooks or libraries this feature depends on ("(nothing yet)" when it did not need any). A criterion about outside data names only what the research confirms and cites its URL. Do not invent a field name, format or limit it did not confirm: write each such detail under the spec's "## Unverified and open questions", with what a person must supply to settle it (a sample payload, a sandbox account, a docs page). The web research:
